<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry Woodward (colonist)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1646–c. 1686</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry Woodward was an English surgeon and explorer, often called the first English settler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>. Left among the local peoples by an expedition in 1666, he learned their languages, was taken by the Spanish to St. Augustine and later joined the Carolina colony at its founding in 1670. As an interpreter and trader he opened commerce with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westo">Westo</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek peoples</a>, laying the foundations of the Carolina Indian trade.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Woodward's early life. He is thought to have been born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbados">Barbados</a>, the son of William Woodward and Esther Martin. In 1666 he went with Captain Robert Sandford's exploration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> coast and volunteered to stay near Port Royal Sound among the Cusabo to learn their language, for which the Lords Proprietors granted him, for the time being, formal possession of the whole country as tenant at will.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Captured by the Spanish in 1667, he was held at St. Augustine for about a year, was well treated, professed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and served as official surgeon, and there learned how the Spanish trade with the native peoples worked. He escaped in 1668 when the privateer Robert Searle raided the town, served as a surgeon on privateers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, was shipwrecked on Nevis in August 1669 and joined the fleet that founded Charleston. From 1670 he led expeditions into the interior, the first Anglo-American colonist to do so, and was the last European to visit Cofitachequi.</p><p>In October 1674 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westo">Westo</a> envoys took him to their town of Hickauhaugau on the Savannah River, where he opened trade. He later made ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a> known as the Savannah, who helped the colony in its war of 1679 against the Westo; by 1680 the Westo had been decimated. Their destruction cost him the favour of the proprietors, who had claimed a monopoly of the Indian trade in 1677, but after he was shot in the head in 1682 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, won a pardon and was made their Indian agent for a fifth of the profits.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the mid-1680s Woodward led a dozen Charles Town traders to the towns of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chattahoochee-river">Chattahoochee River</a>, among them Coweta and Cussita, and through Chickasaw living there opened the way to trade with the Chickasaw. His visit of 1685 drew Spanish expeditions under Lieutenant Governor Antonio Matheos, who in 1686 burned four towns that refused to submit. He is credited with starting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> growing in the colony in 1685, from seed brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> by John Thurber. Woodward died in Charles Town, and his traders took over the business; a large part of the Charleston planter and merchant elite descended from him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry Woodward (colonist)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the English trader <b>Henry Woodward</b> visited in 1670 it was still recognised but much smaller, and it vanished soon after, its people probably merging with the Catawba and other piedmont groups.</blockquote><a href="/state/cofitachequi">Cofitachequi</a></li></ul></section>
